body:
> Again, Godot doesn't do anything to your shader. The shader code is sent directly to the GPU driver's shader compiler. So the problem is with the shader or with the GPU driver. There is nothing we can do in Godot to improve this

@clayjohn 

I understand that. But are you using Windows? I myself don’t usually work on it, but I made an effort and ran it on four different machines. Try running it on Windows and you’ll see that the problem apparently does not depend on the GPU or drivers, contrary to what you’re saying. I thought the same at first, but today I see that it’s not the case.

And I already shared the shader above. I literally checked the image, verified the formats, checked everything, and found that the error is exactly on this line with texelFetch.

You’re wrong to say that you can’t do anything – you just don’t want to. If you wanted, you would at least test the minimal build.

And if you really wanted, here’s the shader again. I’ll provide this part once more so you don’t have to look for it:
```
shader_type spatial;
render_mode depth_prepass_alpha, cull_disabled, diffuse_lambert, specular_toon;

uniform sampler2DArray textureAtlas : filter_nearest_mipmap_anisotropic, source_color, repeat_enable;
uniform sampler3D blockData3d: filter_nearest, repeat_disable;

varying vec3 localPos;
varying vec3 faceDir; // Face direction

//
void vertex() {
    localPos = VERTEX;
    faceDir = CUSTOM0.xyz;
}

void fragment() {
    // Use faceDir to calculate offset
    ivec3 voxelCoord = ivec3(floor(localPos - faceDir * 0.5));
    voxelCoord = clamp(voxelCoord, ivec3(0), ivec3(31)); // Considering chunk size 32

    vec4 idTexel = texelFetch(blockData3d, voxelCoord, 0); // Error occurs here
    int blockId = int(floor(idTexel.r * 255.0 + 0.5));

    if (blockId == 0) {
        ALBEDO = vec3(1, 0, 1); // Shows erroneous spots here
    } else {
        int layer = blockId - 1;

        vec4 sampledColor = texture(textureAtlas, vec3(UV * 8.0, float(layer)));
        ALBEDO = sampledColor.rgb;

        ROUGHNESS = 1.0;
        METALLIC = 0.0;
        SPECULAR = 0.1;

        // General check for front-facing faces
        if (!FRONT_FACING) discard;
    }
}
```

Create the map — I won’t even bother holding back, I’ll show you how to do it:
```rust
/// Creates a 3D texture from block data
fn update3dTexture(&mut self, block_data: &[u8]) 
{
  let chunkSize: i32 = GdChunksManager::chunkSize as i32;

  // Create an array of images for each layer (by Z)
  let mut layers: Array<Gd<Image>> = Array::new();
  for z in 0..GdChunksManager::chunkSize 
  {
    let mut layerData: Vec<u8> = Vec::with_capacity(GdChunksManager::chunkSize2);

    for y in 0..GdChunksManager::chunkSize 
    {
      for x in 0..GdChunksManager::chunkSize 
      {
        let index: usize = x + y * GdChunksManager::chunkSize + z * GdChunksManager::chunkSize2;
        layerData.push(block_data[index]);
      }
    }

    //
    let image: Gd<Image> = Image::create_from_data(
      chunkSize,
      chunkSize,
      false,
      Format::R8,
      &PackedByteArray::from(layerData),
    ).unwrap();
    layers.push(&image);
  }

  // Create the 3D texture
  let mut texture3d: Gd<ImageTexture3D> = ImageTexture3D::new_gd();
  texture3d.create(
    Format::R8,
    chunkSize,
    chunkSize,
    chunkSize,
    false,
    &layers,
  );

  // Set the texture in the material
  let meshInstance: &mut Gd<MeshInstance3D> = self.mesh.as_mut().unwrap();
  let mut material: Gd<ShaderMaterial> = meshInstance.get_material_override().unwrap().cast();
  material.set_shader_parameter("blockData3d", &texture3d.to_variant());
}
```

The exact same thing can be reproduced in pure GDScript.
